  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:139-140  switch_to_next_domain_context
      This code is unrelated to Ssehv/Ssivt. It fixes a real bug: after csr_swap(CSR_SATP, ...) changes the address translation mode (e.g., Sv39 to Sv48), stale TLB entries from the old mode must be invalidated via __sbi_sfence_vma_all(). Without this fence, the previous translation mode's TLB entries could be re-used under the new mode, yielding undefined address translation. The fix is correct: the sfence.vma is issued immediately after the SATP write, which is the required ordering per the RISC-V privilege spec (Section 4.2.1: sfence.vma after SATP changes). Separately, stvec (the supervisor trap vector) is properly saved/restored at line 133 during domain switch — OpenSBI as M-mode firmware saves/restores supervisor CSRs per-domain, including stvec, sie, sip, and the full trap context. Neither Ssehv nor Ssivt are referenced anywhere in this codebase; these are hardware ISA extensions, not firmware features.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_domain_context.c:44-45  struct hart_context
      The 'unsigned long satp' field stores the per-domain SATP value (including the MODE field for address translation mode). Different domains can have different satp.MODE values. The field definition itself is purely structural — it stores what was captured during domain entry context creation. Compliance is verified at the use site (line 139-140) where the SATP swap is immediately followed by a TLB flush, preventing stale TLB entries across mode switches. No Ssehv/Ssivt dependency exists or is violated here.

